We are starting a new project called:

BlueMind Web Service

IMPORTANT:
Do NOT start designing or programming the actual website yet.
Do NOT build pages, sections, buttons, animations, authentication, dashboards, or business features.

For this step I only want the project infrastructure created and fully verified before development begins.

GOAL:
Prepare and verify:

1. Frontend project
2. Backend project
3. GitHub connection
4. Frontend deployment URL
5. Backend deployment URL
6. Frontend → Backend connection
7. Environment variables
8. CORS
9. Health checks
10. Deployment verification

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 — INSPECT FIRST
--------------------------------------------------

Before modifying anything:

- Inspect the current workspace.
- Check whether frontend/backend projects already exist.
- Check Git status.
- Check GitHub remote.
- Check existing package.json files.
- Check existing environment files.
- Check whether Vercel/Render configuration already exists.
- Do not overwrite an existing configuration blindly.

Report what currently exists before making major structural changes.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — PROJECT STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------

If the project is currently empty, prepare a clean structure such as:

BlueMind-Web-Service/
    frontend/
    backend/

Frontend:
- Next.js
- React
- TypeScript

Backend:
Use the existing approved backend technology if one already exists.

If no backend exists yet, create only the smallest clean backend required for connectivity testing.
Do NOT build business logic yet.

Keep the structure clean and production-ready.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — FRONTEND
--------------------------------------------------

Prepare the frontend so it can run locally and be deployed.

Do NOT design the website.

For now the frontend only needs a minimal temporary test screen that confirms:

BlueMind Web Service
Frontend is running
Backend connection status: Connected / Failed

This temporary screen will later be deleted when real design development begins.

The frontend must use an environment variable for the backend URL.

Example:

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

Never hard-code the production backend URL throughout the application.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — BACKEND HEALTH ENDPOINT
--------------------------------------------------

Create or verify a very small public health endpoint such as:

GET /api/health

Expected response:

{
  "success": true,
  "service": "BlueMind Web Service API",
  "status": "healthy"
}

This endpoint must:

- require no authentication
- contain no private information
- expose no secrets
- expose no database credentials
- expose no environment variables
- return quickly

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — FRONTEND → BACKEND CONNECTION
--------------------------------------------------

Connect the temporary frontend test screen to:

GET /api/health

The frontend must visibly confirm whether the backend is reachable.

Test:

Frontend → Backend → valid response.

Do not consider the setup finished just because each service works separately.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 6 — CORS
--------------------------------------------------

Configure CORS safely.

Allow:

- the frontend production URL
- frontend localhost development URL

Do NOT use unrestricted wildcard CORS in production unless technically required and justified.

Keep allowed origins configurable through environment variables.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 7 — GITHUB
--------------------------------------------------

Verify that the project is connected to the correct GitHub repository.

Check:

git status
git branch
git remote -v

Make sure:

- secrets are not committed
- .env files containing secrets are ignored
- .env.example exists where useful
- node_modules is ignored
- build files are ignored appropriately

Do not push secrets to GitHub.

If a GitHub remote already exists, preserve it.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 8 — FRONTEND DEPLOYMENT
--------------------------------------------------

Use Vercel for the frontend if Vercel is already authorized/available for this project.

Create or verify a production frontend deployment.

I want a real HTTPS URL.

Example form:

https://<project>.vercel.app

Do not invent or report a URL unless the deployment actually exists and responds successfully.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 9 — BACKEND DEPLOYMENT
--------------------------------------------------

Use Render for the backend if Render is already authorized/available for this project.

Create or verify the backend production service.

I want a real HTTPS backend URL.

Example form:

https://<project>.onrender.com

Then verify:

https://<backend-url>/api/health

must return HTTP 200 with the expected JSON.

Do not invent deployment URLs.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 10 — ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
--------------------------------------------------

Configure the production frontend with the real backend URL.

For example:

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://<real-backend-url>

Configure backend allowed origins using the real frontend URL.

Keep production and local development values separated.

Do not expose server secrets to NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 11 — TEST EVERYTHING
--------------------------------------------------

Perform actual tests.

LOCAL TESTS:

1. Frontend starts successfully.
2. Backend starts successfully.
3. Backend health endpoint returns HTTP 200.
4. Frontend successfully calls local backend.
5. Invalid backend path returns expected 404.
6. No obvious console/runtime errors.

PRODUCTION TESTS:

1. Open production frontend URL.
2. Confirm HTTP 200.
3. Open production backend /api/health.
4. Confirm HTTP 200.
5. Confirm frontend successfully reaches production backend.
6. Check CORS.
7. Check browser console for errors.
8. Check frontend deployment logs.
9. Check backend deployment logs where access is available.
10. Confirm HTTPS is enabled.

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 12 — SECURITY CHECK
--------------------------------------------------

Before reporting completion, verify:

- no API keys in frontend code
- no credentials committed to GitHub
- no .env production file committed
- no database URI exposed
- no internal stack traces returned by /api/health
- no unrestricted production CORS unless justified
- no unnecessary public endpoints
- environment variables are configured correctly

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 13 — DO NOT START REAL DEVELOPMENT
--------------------------------------------------

STOP after infrastructure verification.

Do not start:

- Home page
- UI design
- navigation
- animations
- forms
- authentication
- database features
- portfolio
- contact page
- admin panel
- any real product features

We will start those only after I approve the infrastructure.

